title Season G fourth primitive — Poly1305 (RFC 8439) in pure Rye, RFC + std.crypto parity GREEN
prompt Recursion loop (baton prin recur): keep taking agent-doable laps Lindy-first crux-first, Rye prioritized; send each finished increment as its own signed round; stop at the custody gates.
think Three primitives stand GREEN (BLAKE2b, SHA-512, ChaCha20). The ChaCha20 round named the next Season G crux plainly: Poly1305 completes the ChaCha20-Poly1305 AEAD, and it is more tractable than Ed25519's edwards25519 field/group arithmetic (which still wants its own design round). Poly1305 is Monocypher's one-time authenticator, pure integer arithmetic over the prime 2^130 − 5 — no big-integer library once the accumulator is carried in five 26-bit limbs, so every limb product fits one 64-bit word. Genuinely buildable in authored Rye this round.
think Provable now against two references: the RFC 8439 §2.5.2 known-answer (the tag for "Cryptographic Forum Research Group" under the RFC's one-time key) and Zig's std.crypto Poly1305 as an independent oracle. The primitive calls no std.crypto; std is used only inside the selftest.
think Authored the §2.5 clamp of the coefficient r, the message-add / multiply-by-r / mod-2^130−5 reduction per 16-byte block (each over-130 carry folded back as ×5, since 2^130 ≡ 5), the full carry and single conditional subtraction of the prime at finalize (branchless via a mask so the choice stays data-independent), the five-limb-to-four-word fold, and the pad s added mod 2^128 to yield the 16-byte tag. The final partial block copies into a zeroed buffer with a single 0x01 marker byte — no @memcpy, a bounded copy loop.
obs GREEN on the first metal pass — the RFC §2.5.2 known-answer plus Zig std.crypto parity across seven sizes (empty, 1, 15, 16, 17, 34, 256) would have flagged any wrong clamp constant, fold, or carry, and it stayed GREEN. tame_style_check bans clean; the new file appears in none of the ratchet lists (no @memcpy, snake_case, functions under 70 lines, asserts present).
obs Self-approved under the season's opening design read (20260815-180728) and the decision-wave doc (20260815-175524), which name Poly1305 as the primitive that completes the ChaCha20-Poly1305 AEAD after ChaCha20 — no new design round, no custody gate near a keyless MAC run on the RFC's public one-time vector. A MAC authenticates; it does not sign with an identity, so signing-with-identity-key stays the untouched gate.
obs The 64-bit products in absorb are a genuine arithmetic need (two 26-bit limbs multiply to ~52 bits, summed across five terms), not a wire width — commented as such so the u64 reads as honest under the explicit-width discipline.
loom witness=crypto_poly1305_witness rfc_vectors=1 std_parity_inputs=7 block_bytes=16 limbs=5 result=GREEN
file crypto/poly1305.rye the authored Poly1305 primitive — init/clamp, absorb (message-add, multiply-by-r, mod-2^130−5 reduction), finalize (full carry, conditional prime subtraction, pad add), one-shot mac; bounded input, contract asserts on every function, no std.crypto in the primitive
file tools/crypto_poly1305_witness.rish builds and runs the selftest; asserts the GREEN crypto-poly1305 line; RFC 8439 §2.5.2 known-answer + std.crypto parity proven on metal across seven block edges
recommend keep-going — four primitives now stand GREEN (BLAKE2b · SHA-512 · ChaCha20 · Poly1305). The ChaCha20-Poly1305 AEAD is now composable from two GREEN Rye halves (encrypt-then-MAC with the AAD/ciphertext padding and length trailer, its own round). The remaining Season G crux toward the Lotus signed carry is Ed25519's edwards25519 field/group arithmetic, which wants its own design round; signing with the maintainer's identity key stays the custody gate.
